<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<widget xmlns="http://www.w3.org/ns/widgets" xmlns:tizen="http://tizen.org/ns/widgets"
        id="http://screentinker.com/player" version="2.0.9" viewmodes="maximized">
    <tizen:application id="ScrnTinkr1.ScreenTinker" package="ScrnTinkr1" required_version="2.4"/>
    <tizen:profile name="tv"/>
    <name>ScreenTinker</name>
    <author email="support@screentinker.net">ScreenTinker</author>
    <description>ScreenTinker digital signage player</description>
    <icon src="icon.png"/>
    <content src="index.html"/>

    <!--
      ⚠️ THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO <tizen:content-security-policy> HERE. #330.

      One was declared in 9eee571 and shipped in 2.0.0 through 2.0.7. It BLACK-SCREENED an OM55B on
      Tizen 5.0 / SSSP v6 (Chromium 63): the app installed, the panel menu still responded, and
      nothing rendered. Proven by bisect on the panel, not reasoned about. A control build identical
      to 2.0.7 except with this one element deleted rendered correctly; 1.9.24, which never had the
      element, has always worked.

      WHAT REMOVING IT COSTS. player.js renderBundle mounts a CACHED offline bundle as f.srcdoc, a
      srcdoc frame inherits this document policy, and lib/bundle-inline.js turns a bundle EXTERNAL
      <script src>, <img>, fonts and CSS url() into data: URIs. The WRT default does not grant
      data:, so those specific resources drop. NOT the whole page: bundle-inline.js flattens
      stylesheets into inline <style> and never rewrites inline <script>, and the WRT default
      permits unsafe-inline for both. Layout and inline JS survive. It only affects a SECOND play of
      a revision already warmed into wgt-private; the first play and every zone bundle use f.src =
      and never consulted a policy here.

      ⚠️ WHAT THE ORIGINAL COMMENT GOT WRONG, so nobody repeats it. It claimed the default renders
      a "styled, DEAD page", citing a measurement taken against the DASHBOARD policy in
      server/server.js. That policy has no unsafe-inline in script-src; the WRT default does. The
      dashboard result therefore says nothing about the axis that decides this, and the element was
      shipped to a fleet on the strength of it.

      ⚠️ THE POLICY ALSO BROKE THE MEDIA CACHE, which is the reason a corrected policy is not the
      fix. img-src and media-src were declared without file:, while media-cache.js hands the DOM a
      file:// URI from toURI() and player.js contentUrl() prefers it with no network fallback. A
      relaxed script-src would have booted the app and then black-screened it on any cached item,
      on a 5 second skipSoon retry loop. Moving the failure, not removing it.

      ⚠️ BEFORE ANY POLICY GOES BACK IN, measure three things ON A PANEL:
        1. The real origin of this document. Three files here assert app:// and none verifies it.
           If WRT serves the package over file://, the origin is opaque, self matches NOTHING, and
           every <script src="js/..."> in index.html is blocked. That predicts the exact #330
           symptom and would mean no self-based policy can ever be safe here.
        2. index.html loads $WEBAPIS and $B2BAPIS from OUTSIDE the package. No hand-written
           script-src can match those, so any explicit policy silently removes the Samsung B2B
           surface device-control.js depends on.
        3. Ship a securitypolicyviolation listener FIRST. There is none anywhere in this repo, which
           is why #330 produced a black screen with nothing in any log.

      The better fix for the data: dependency is to stop needing a policy at all: mount the cached
      render by its wgt-private file:// URI instead of srcdoc, so it becomes a separate document
      with no inherited policy. The frame already carries sandbox="allow-scripts" with no
      allow-same-origin, so it keeps an opaque origin either way.

      ⚠️ REMOVING THIS NEEDS A NEW .wgt. There is no in-app OTA for this player, so a panel in the
      field keeps the 2.0.x policy until somebody reinstalls it.
    -->

    <!-- Landscape signage, no context menu, allow background, keep app full-screen -->
    <tizen:setting screen-orientation="landscape" context-menu="disable"
                   background-support="enable" encryption="disable"
                   install-location="auto" hwkey-event="enable"/>

    <feature name="http://tizen.org/feature/screen.size.all"/>

    <!-- Allow the player to reach any signage server + load remote media / YouTube -->
    <access origin="*" subdomains="true"/>
    <tizen:allow-navigation>*</tizen:allow-navigation>

    <tizen:privilege name="http://tizen.org/privilege/internet"/>
    <tizen:privilege name="http://tizen.org/privilege/application.launch"/>
    <tizen:privilege name="http://tizen.org/privilege/display"/>
    <!-- tizen.tvaudiocontrol: sets the TV's OWN volume, which is what reaches AVPlay video on the
         hardware plane. Without this privilege the API throws SecurityError and the player falls
         back to per-element media volume, which cannot touch that plane. -->
    <tizen:privilege name="http://tizen.org/privilege/tv.audio"/>
    <!-- Offline media cache (js/media-cache.js). Content is written to wgt-private, which is the
         store Tizen documents as surviving reboots and app updates — without these the player can
         hold a playlist through an outage but none of the media it points at, which from the floor
         is the same as holding nothing. -->
    <tizen:privilege name="http://tizen.org/privilege/filesystem.read"/>
    <tizen:privilege name="http://tizen.org/privilege/filesystem.write"/>
    <tizen:privilege name="http://developer.samsung.com/privilege/network.public"/>

    <!-- #125: Samsung B2B fleet control (reboot / panel power via b2bcontrol /
         systemcontrol). These are PARTNER-level privileges: they only take effect
         when the .wgt is signed with a Samsung Partner distributor certificate on a
         real SSSP panel. On unsigned / URL-Launcher / web / consumer-TV builds they
         are ignored (not fatal) — the APIs are simply absent and device-control.js
         reports "unsupported". -->
    <tizen:privilege name="http://developer.samsung.com/privilege/b2bcontrol"/>
    <tizen:privilege name="http://developer.samsung.com/privilege/systemcontrol"/>
</widget>
